# VLSM Subnetting Breakdown

**Base allocation:** `41.41.0.0/16` (65,536 addresses)

The design follows a top-down VLSM approach: the largest, most stable requirements (site LANs) are carved out first as `/24`s, then progressively smaller requirements (nested sub-sites, then point-to-point WAN links) are carved out of the remaining space, right-sized to how many hosts each segment actually needs.

## Step 1 — Site LANs (carved from `41.41.0.0/16` as `/24`s)

| Subnet | CIDR | Usable Hosts | Assigned To |
|---|---|---|---|
| `41.41.0.0/24` | /24 | 254 | **MOH** LAN |
| `41.41.1.0/24` | /24 | 254 | **WHC** LAN |
| `41.41.2.0/24` | /24 | 254 | **CSC** LAN |
| `41.41.3.0/24` | /24 | 254 | **DDU** LAN |
| `41.41.4.0/24` | /24 | 254 | Reserved for **DPU/RPP** (subnetted further, see Step 2) |
| `41.41.5.0/24` | /24 | 254 | Reserved for **WAN backbone** (subnetted further, see Step 3) |

## Step 2 — DPU / RPP nested sub-sites (carved from `41.41.4.0/24`)

The DPU site needed a smaller LAN, and within it, a further sub-site (RPP) needed an even smaller LAN — a classic VLSM "subnet-within-a-subnet" scenario.

```
41.41.4.0/24
├── 41.41.4.0/25    → DPU LAN (126 usable hosts)
└── 41.41.4.128/25  → further split:
    ├── 41.41.4.128/26 → RPP LAN (62 usable hosts)
    └── 41.41.4.192/26 → reserved / unused
```

| Subnet | CIDR | Mask | Usable Hosts | Assigned To |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `41.41.4.0/25` | /25 | `255.255.255.128` | 126 | **DPU** LAN |
| `41.41.4.128/26` | /26 | `255.255.255.192` | 62 | **RPP** LAN |
| `41.41.4.192/26` | /26 | `255.255.255.192` | 62 | Reserved (unused) |

## Step 3 — WAN point-to-point links (carved from `41.41.5.0/24` as `/30`s)

Point-to-point serial links only need 2 usable host addresses, so each is subnetted down to a `/30` (4 addresses total, 2 usable). This is the classic VLSM efficiency win: instead of burning a full `/24` (254 usable) on a link that only ever needs 2 addresses, six `/30`s fit inside a single `/24` with room to spare.

| Subnet | CIDR | Mask | Usable Hosts | Link |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `41.41.5.0/30` | /30 | `255.255.255.252` | 2 | MOH ↔ WHC |
| `41.41.5.4/30` | /30 | `255.255.255.252` | 2 | WHC ↔ DDU |
| `41.41.5.8/30` | /30 | `255.255.255.252` | 2 | DDU ↔ RPP |
| `41.41.5.12/30` | /30 | `255.255.255.252` | 2 | CSC ↔ DPU |
| `41.41.5.16/30` | /30 | `255.255.255.252` | 2 | MOH ↔ CSC (shared backbone) |
| `41.41.5.20/30` | /30 | `255.255.255.252` | 2 | WHC ↔ CSC (backup link) |

`41.41.5.24/30` through `41.41.5.252/30` remain unused/reserved for future WAN expansion.

## Why VLSM (instead of one fixed mask everywhere)

A single fixed subnet mask across the whole `/16` would either:
- waste hundreds of addresses on 2-host WAN links if a large mask like `/24` were used everywhere, or
- fail to provide enough addresses for site LANs if a small mask like `/30` were used everywhere.

VLSM lets each segment use exactly the mask it needs — `/24` for LANs with ~250 hosts, `/25`/`/26` for smaller nested LANs, and `/30` for WAN links — which is why the addressing tree branches to different prefix lengths at different points in the diagram above.
